      "summary": "A Phoenix client patch corrects prompt-variable handling; its command-line package picks up the same fix through a dependency update.",
      "body": [
        "Phoenix is an AI observability platform whose client library helps applications work with prompts. A client patch fixes variable substitution that could change literal dollar sequences in supplied text. That matters when a prompt includes shell syntax or questions about programming: the text inserted into the prompt should match the value the application provided.",
        "Previously, the formatting utility passed each variable’s value directly to JavaScript’s replaceAll function. That function can interpret dollar sequences as replacement instructions. The patch instead supplies a function that returns the value, preserving it as literal text. The implementation still loops through the configured replacements; the change is how each replacement value reaches the string operation.",
        "The accompanying tests cover a Mustache-style prompt containing a Bash question with two dollar signs, and a brace-style prompt whose value contains a dollar sign followed by an ampersand. Both checks expect the supplied text to survive formatting. The stable command-line release also updates its client dependency to include this correction. The source reports no measured effect on evaluation results."
      ],
      "format": "full",
      "takeaway": "For Phoenix users passing dollar-containing text into prompts: include literal-value preservation in upgrade checks, including workflows using the command-line package.",
